# MOD-08 Reporting Suite — configuration
# Copy to .env (or export in your shell) and adjust. Every value has a
# local-dev default; set real values before deploying anywhere.
#
# The query row cap and time limit are NOT environment variables — they
# are declarative code config in server/query-policy.ts (see README).

# HTTP port of the API / production server
PORT=3008

# Path to the module's OWN metadata database (reports, charts, schedules,
# run history). Created automatically. Never your business data.
DATABASE_PATH=./data.db

# Path to the SQLite database you want to report ON. Opened strictly
# READ-ONLY — the module never writes to it. If you leave this unset, a
# deterministic demo database (a year of sales data) is generated at
# ./source.db on first start so everything works out of the box. If you
# SET it and the file does not exist, the server refuses to start.
SOURCE_DB_PATH=./source.db

# Restrict every report to the source's published report_* VIEWS — the
# reporting contract (docs/REPORTING-CONTRACT.md). "true" turns it on;
# anything else (including unset) leaves it OFF, which is the default.
#
# On: /api/source lists only the source's published views, and a query
# that reads anything else is refused at save time and at run time. The
# check is on the objects a query READS, so none of these get round it:
# an alias, a CTE, a subquery, a comma-join, a parenthesised table list
# like FROM (customers), a quoted identifier, or a table that merely
# happens to be NAMED report_something. The source's own catalog decides,
# and it lists views only — the prefix is a naming convention, not the
# boundary. CTEs keep working normally.
#
# Off: the whole source schema is queryable, which is what you want when
# this is pointed at an arbitrary customer database. The read-only
# connection is the safety guarantee either way; this is about scope.
#
# You normally do NOT set this by hand in a generated stack:
# deploy/provision.mjs emits SOURCE_VIEWS_ONLY=true automatically when
# --source-db names a module that publishes a report_* contract.
# SOURCE_VIEWS_ONLY=false

# Directory scheduled/manual export runs write their CSV files into.
# Created automatically; gitignored.
EXPORTS_DIR=./exports

# How often the in-process scheduler checks for due schedules (seconds).
SCHEDULER_TICK_SECONDS=60

# Admin credentials — the single staff login for the app.
# Local-dev defaults are admin / admin. CHANGE THESE IN PRODUCTION.
ADMIN_USERNAME=admin
ADMIN_PASSWORD=admin

# Secret used to sign the session cookie AND chart embed tokens.
# Any long random string. Generate one with: openssl rand -hex 32
# Rotating it logs everyone out and invalidates existing embed URLs.
SESSION_SECRET=dev-secret-change-me

# Session lifetime in hours (default 12)
SESSION_TTL_HOURS=12

# Set to "true" behind HTTPS so the session cookie is marked Secure
COOKIE_SECURE=false

# ── Platform Services (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────
# When AUDIT_URL is set, key changes are recorded on PS-07 Audit Log. Unset
# means standalone (no outbound calls).
# AUDIT_URL=http://localhost:4007
# PLATFORM_SERVICE_TOKEN=dev-service-token

# ── Hardening (defaults shown; 0 disables a limiter) ───────────────────
# Security headers are always sent. These knobs tune the rest of
# server/hardening.ts: per-IP rate limits, the CORS allowlist and the
# socket timeout. HSTS is on automatically when NODE_ENV=production.
# A value that is not a non-negative number (a typo, or an empty string
# from an unset variable interpolated into a compose file) falls back to
# the default rather than silently switching the limiter off.
# RATE_LIMIT_RPM=600
# LOGIN_RATE_LIMIT_RPM=20
# CORS_ORIGINS=
# REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=30000

# Reverse proxies in front of this service. Behind the deploy stack's Caddy
# that is 1 — without it every request carries the proxy's address, so the
# per-IP limits above throttle all clients as one. Leave it at 0 when the
# service is reachable directly: a client could otherwise forge the header.
# TRUST_PROXY=0
